<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Vestini</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 302 BCE (approx.) · capital Pinna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sabellic people of the Gran Sasso and the Aterno valley in Abruzzo, whose territory produced the famous Warrior of Capestrano statue.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Vestini lived in the high valleys around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-sasso">Gran Sasso</a> and the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aterno-pescara">Aterno</a>, reaching the Adriatic coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penne-abruzzo">Pinna</a> (Penne). Ancient writers counted them among the Sabellian peoples; their culture developed locally from the early 1st millennium BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in scattered villages, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> and towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penne-abruzzo">Pinna</a>, Aveia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peltuinum">Peltuinum</a>, keeping flocks that moved between mountain and lowland pastures. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warrior-of-capestrano">Warrior of Capestrano</a>, a life-size limestone statue of the 6th century BCE with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-picene-language">South Picene</a> inscription, was found in a Vestine cemetery and is the most famous work of Italic sculpture. Their political organisation is otherwise poorly known.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samnite-league">Samnites</a>, the Vestini were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 325 BCE and made an alliance around 302 BCE. They joined the Italian revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> and were defeated in 89 BCE, then received citizenship and were absorbed into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vestini.jpg" alt="Vestini" width="320" height="253"><figcaption>Image: Philipp Clüver · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:06_-_Piceni_et_Vestinorum,_Pelignorum,_Marrucinorum;_ac_Frentanorum_agri_descriptio,_1624_-_Philip_Cl%C3%BCver.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 302 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pinna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sabellic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oscan-Umbrian dialect</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-451&amp;focus=vestini" aria-label="Vestini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5017 668 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vestini lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5337 924L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5337 924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5337 924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pinna. <a href="/atlas?year=-451&amp;focus=vestini">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

